Algorithm:

Step 1: Store the exponent N in a long variable n to handle large negative values.
Step 2: If n is negative, convert the base to its reciprocal (1/x) and make n positive.
Step 3: Initialize ans = 1 to store the final result.
Step 4: Initialize curr = x to represent the current power of the base.
Step 5: Start a loop while n > 0.
Step 6: If n is even, square the current value (curr = curr * curr).
Step 7: If n is odd, multiply ans with curr, then square curr.
Step 8: Divide n by 2 in every iteration to reduce the exponent.
Step 9: Continue the process until n becomes 0.
Step 10: Return ans as the final power value.

Time Complexity: O(log n)
Space Complexity: O(1)


-----> Code <-----

class Solution {
    public double myPow(double x, int N) {
        long n=N;
        if(n<0){
            x=1/x;
            n=-(n);
        }
        double ans=1;
        double curr=x;
        while(n>0){
            if(n%2==0){
                curr*=curr;
            }else{
                ans*=curr;
                curr*=curr;
            }
            n=n/2;
        }
        return ans;
    }
}
